1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,009
Pantrix.

2
00:00:01,009 --> 00:00:04,570
A DevSecOps project by Dhruv Sharma.

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:11,183
This video starts with the presentation and then walks through the running system.

4
00:00:12,583 --> 00:00:15,512
Part one: the presentation.

5
00:00:16,916 --> 00:00:22,142
Pantrix helps community food banks track donated stock and its expiry dates, so

6
00:00:22,142 --> 00:00:24,955
food is handed out before it spoils.

7
00:00:24,955 --> 00:00:30,180
It is built as a complete delivery pipeline, from a developer laptop to

8
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:31,788
a monitored Kubernetes cluster.

9
00:00:33,188 --> 00:00:39,624
Volunteers log donations, see what expires first, record hand-outs to partner organisations, and

10
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:42,594
the stock count goes down automatically.

11
00:00:42,594 --> 00:00:49,030
Expired stock cannot be handed out, and over-asking is rejected by the API.

12
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:56,217
These screenshots come from a real create, edit, hand-out and delete flow, captured

13
00:00:56,217 --> 00:01:01,113
against the running application, on desktop and on a phone-sized screen.

14
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:10,490
The stack follows the course reference: React with Vite, FastAPI, PostgreSQL with SQLAlchemy

15
00:01:10,490 --> 00:01:18,467
and Alembic, pytest, Docker, GitHub Actions, Trivy, the GitHub Container Registry, Terraform for

16
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:23,990
AWS, Kubernetes with Helm, Argo CD, Prometheus and Grafana.

17
00:01:25,389 --> 00:01:28,191
Traffic enters through the ingress controller.

18
00:01:28,191 --> 00:01:34,262
Slash goes to the frontend Nginx pods, slash API goes to the FastAPI

19
00:01:34,262 --> 00:01:36,596
pods, which talk to PostgreSQL.

20
00:01:36,596 --> 00:01:42,667
Prometheus scrapes every backend pod, Grafana shows the dashboard, and Argo CD keeps

21
00:01:42,667 --> 00:01:45,935
a second environment in sync with Git.

22
00:01:47,335 --> 00:01:54,190
Three tables, created by an Alembic migration: donors, stock items and distributions.

23
00:01:54,190 --> 00:02:01,617
The API offers full create, read, update and delete for items and donors,

24
00:02:01,617 --> 00:02:06,187
plus statistics, distributions, health, readiness and metrics endpoints.

25
00:02:07,602 --> 00:02:16,003
Thirty-one backend tests reach ninety-six percent coverage against a throw-away database built by the real migrations.

26
00:02:16,003 --> 00:02:21,253
Ten frontend tests cover rendering, API failures and form validation.

27
00:02:21,253 --> 00:02:26,503
Ruff, Bandit, pip-audit, ESLint and npm audit run as well.

28
00:02:27,903 --> 00:02:30,007
Both images are multi-stage builds.

29
00:02:30,007 --> 00:02:35,476
The backend runs as user ten thousand and one, the frontend as user

30
00:02:35,476 --> 00:02:38,842
one hundred and one on an unprivileged Nginx.

31
00:02:38,842 --> 00:02:45,574
Docker Compose starts PostgreSQL, the API and the UI with health checks and a persistent volume.

32
00:02:46,974 --> 00:02:50,409
The pipeline runs on every push to main.

33
00:02:50,409 --> 00:02:54,273
Tests, linting, secret scanning and infrastructure checks run first.

34
00:02:54,273 --> 00:02:59,855
Only then are both images built, checked for a non-root user, scanned by

35
00:02:59,855 --> 00:03:05,437
Trivy, and pushed to the GitHub Container Registry tagged with the commit SHA.

36
00:03:05,437 --> 00:03:10,160
The first real GitHub run happens once the repository is pushed.

37
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:13,234
Trivy found real problems.

38
00:03:13,234 --> 00:03:18,627
The first backend image failed the gate with four fixable high severity vulnerabilities

39
00:03:18,627 --> 00:03:22,776
in libraries vendored by pip, which the API never uses.

40
00:03:22,776 --> 00:03:29,415
The fix removed pip from the runtime image, and the gate now passes without suppressing anything.

41
00:03:30,815 --> 00:03:35,995
The Terraform describes a VPC with two public and two private subnets in

42
00:03:35,995 --> 00:03:40,776
the Mumbai region and an EKS cluster with a managed node group.

43
00:03:40,776 --> 00:03:46,753
It was not applied to a real AWS account, because no valid credentials were available.

44
00:03:46,753 --> 00:03:51,535
Instead it was planned, applied and destroyed against the Moto AWS emulator.

45
00:03:51,535 --> 00:03:53,925
A real apply is still pending.

46
00:03:55,325 --> 00:04:01,411
Helm deploys two replicas of each tier with hardened pods, an Ingress, and autoscalers.

47
00:04:01,411 --> 00:04:07,932
Under generated load, the horizontal pod autoscaler took the backend from two to six pods.

48
00:04:09,332 --> 00:04:11,705
Building and deploying are separate.

49
00:04:11,705 --> 00:04:15,501
Continuous integration only builds, scans and publishes images.

50
00:04:15,501 --> 00:04:21,671
Deployment is a Git commit that changes the image tag, and Argo CD

51
00:04:21,671 --> 00:04:24,043
reconciles the cluster to match.

52
00:04:25,443 --> 00:04:27,471
FastAPI exposes Prometheus metrics.

53
00:04:27,471 --> 00:04:34,062
Every backend pod is a target in state up, and the Grafana dashboard

54
00:04:34,062 --> 00:04:40,145
shows request rate, error ratio, latency percentiles, business events and pod resources.

55
00:04:41,545 --> 00:04:47,069
Five troubleshooting drills were scripted and run: a missing image tag, a Service

56
00:04:47,069 --> 00:04:52,593
with no endpoints, a readiness failure, a migration failure, and a bad release

57
00:04:52,593 --> 00:04:55,142
that was rolled back without downtime.

58
00:04:55,142 --> 00:05:00,241
One drill exposed a real Nginx startup bug, which was then fixed.

59
00:05:01,645 --> 00:05:06,660
Two items are pending external access: the first GitHub Actions run with the

60
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,361
registry push, and a real AWS apply.

61
00:05:09,361 --> 00:05:15,147
Everything else in this video is backed by real output in the docs evidence folder.

62
00:05:16,547 --> 00:05:19,761
Part two: the working application.

63
00:05:21,161 --> 00:05:25,246
This is the live application through the Kubernetes Ingress.

64
00:05:25,246 --> 00:05:27,969
The shelf is sorted by expiry.

65
00:05:27,969 --> 00:05:34,776
A new donation is logged, edited, partly handed out to a shelter, and finally deleted.

66
00:05:34,776 --> 00:05:40,676
Every action calls the FastAPI backend, and the data is stored in PostgreSQL.

67
00:05:40,676 --> 00:05:44,307
The layout also adapts to a phone-sized screen.

68
00:06:04,561 --> 00:06:07,907
The same API is documented in Swagger.

69
00:06:07,907 --> 00:06:14,599
Here the statistics endpoint is executed live, followed by the raw Prometheus metrics endpoint.

70
00:06:20,361 --> 00:06:23,195
Part three: the codebase.

71
00:06:24,595 --> 00:06:33,348
The repository holds the backend, the frontend, the Helm chart, Kubernetes manifests, Terraform,

72
00:06:33,348 --> 00:06:42,102
GitOps desired state, monitoring, troubleshooting drills, scripts, workflows, documentation and the demo material.

73
00:06:43,502 --> 00:06:49,305
The distribution endpoint locks the stock row, rejects expired stock and over-asking with

74
00:06:49,305 --> 00:06:53,769
a conflict response, and decrements the quantity in one transaction.

75
00:06:55,169 --> 00:06:57,558
Liveness never touches the database.

76
00:06:57,558 --> 00:07:04,726
Readiness does, so a pod without a database stops receiving traffic but is not restarted.

77
00:07:04,726 --> 00:07:08,071
Prometheus instrumentation is added in one line.

78
00:07:09,471 --> 00:07:14,753
In the Helm chart, migrations run in an init container before the API

79
00:07:14,753 --> 00:07:18,409
starts, with an advisory lock so replicas never race.

80
00:07:18,409 --> 00:07:22,878
Every container runs as non-root with a read-only root file system.

81
00:07:24,278 --> 00:07:27,247
Part four: tests and Docker.

82
00:07:28,647 --> 00:07:32,611
Pytest runs thirty-one tests, all passing.

83
00:07:34,014 --> 00:07:41,637
The frontend passes ESLint, Prettier, ten Vitest tests, npm audit, and the production build.

84
00:07:43,047 --> 00:07:48,674
Docker Compose builds both images and waits until PostgreSQL, the backend and the

85
00:07:48,674 --> 00:07:50,405
frontend are all healthy.

86
00:07:51,814 --> 00:07:58,847
Both containers run as non-root users, confirmed from the image configuration and from the running process.

87
00:08:00,247 --> 00:08:04,306
Part five: continuous integration and delivery.

88
00:08:05,714 --> 00:08:09,083
This is the real job graph of the workflow.

89
00:08:09,083 --> 00:08:13,950
Images are only pushed after every gate passes, and they are tagged with

90
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:15,821
the commit SHA, never latest.

91
00:08:17,222 --> 00:08:21,942
The workflow files were validated with actionlint, with no problems found.

92
00:08:21,942 --> 00:08:25,375
Every action is pinned to a commit SHA.

93
00:08:26,788 --> 00:08:32,009
The live pipeline run and the published packages will be recorded once the

94
00:08:32,009 --> 00:08:33,615
repository is on GitHub.

95
00:08:33,615 --> 00:08:37,631
No pipeline result is shown here, because none exists yet.

96
00:08:39,031 --> 00:08:42,710
Part six: Terraform and AWS.

97
00:08:44,110 --> 00:08:47,605
This is the design described by the Terraform code.

98
00:08:47,605 --> 00:08:51,489
It has not been provisioned in a real AWS account.

99
00:08:52,888 --> 00:08:56,567
Terraform initialises and validates successfully.

100
00:08:57,967 --> 00:09:04,566
A plan against real AWS stops immediately, because the credentials on this machine are invalid.

101
00:09:04,566 --> 00:09:06,765
That is the real blocker.

102
00:09:08,167 --> 00:09:13,991
Against the Moto AWS emulator, the plan resolves sixty-two resources.

103
00:09:15,401 --> 00:09:22,001
In the emulator, apply created fifty-four of them, including the VPC, subnets, NAT

104
00:09:22,001 --> 00:09:29,110
gateway, EKS cluster and node group, before reaching APIs the emulator does not implement.

105
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:31,648
Destroy then removed all fifty-four.

106
00:09:31,648 --> 00:09:34,187
Nothing was created in AWS.

107
00:09:35,601 --> 00:09:40,889
Part seven: Kubernetes, Helm, the Ingress and Argo CD.

108
00:09:42,301 --> 00:09:49,349
The Helm release runs two backend pods, two frontend pods and PostgreSQL, all running.

109
00:09:50,749 --> 00:09:57,667
ClusterIP services, the Ingress for pantrix dot localtest dot me, and the autoscalers.

110
00:09:59,082 --> 00:10:04,432
Helm shows the release history, including a failed bad release and its rollback,

111
00:10:04,432 --> 00:10:06,490
and the Helm test passes.

112
00:10:07,890 --> 00:10:12,826
Under load, CPU reached almost four hundred percent of the target and the

113
00:10:12,826 --> 00:10:16,243
autoscaler scaled the backend from two to six pods.

114
00:10:17,657 --> 00:10:22,373
Argo CD shows the dev application synced and healthy, deployed from a Git

115
00:10:22,373 --> 00:10:24,550
commit that sets the image SHA.

116
00:10:37,990 --> 00:10:43,084
Every promotion is a commit to the GitOps values file, and Argo CD

117
00:10:43,084 --> 00:10:45,043
reconciles the cluster to it.

118
00:10:46,457 --> 00:10:48,991
Part eight: monitoring.

119
00:10:50,391 --> 00:10:55,723
Prometheus shows every backend pod as a target in state up, a live

120
00:10:55,723 --> 00:10:58,594
request-rate query, and the Pantrix alert rules.

121
00:11:04,871 --> 00:11:10,986
The Grafana dashboard updates live with request rate, error ratio, latency percentiles, stock

122
00:11:10,986 --> 00:11:13,809
events, and pod CPU and memory.

123
00:11:27,911 --> 00:11:30,745
Part nine: troubleshooting.

124
00:11:32,145 --> 00:11:38,137
A Service whose selector matches no pods has no endpoints, so requests are refused.

125
00:11:38,137 --> 00:11:44,557
Comparing the selector with the pod labels finds the cause, and correcting it restores traffic.

126
00:11:45,957 --> 00:11:51,293
A release with a missing image fails, but the old pods keep serving,

127
00:11:51,293 --> 00:11:53,345
so every request still succeeds.

128
00:11:53,345 --> 00:11:56,629
Helm rollback returns to the last good revision.

129
00:11:58,030 --> 00:11:59,344
That is Pantrix.

130
00:11:59,344 --> 00:12:04,161
Real output for every claim is in the docs evidence folder.

131
00:12:04,161 --> 00:12:05,913
Thank you for watching.

